WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.120
Speaker 1: OK, next question today.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.640
Does classical conservatism still exist?

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:07.950
This is an interesting question.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:11.861
You know, when we talk about classical conservatism
in the United States, understand that the

00:00:11.861 --> 00:00:18.400
idea of conservatism, the roots of conservatism,
go back actually to the French Revolution

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:25.440
when you had conservatives who were often
French intellectuals saying they wanted to

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:35.480
sort of go back to the previous way that France
was organized rather than the post revolution.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:40.140
France, when we say classical conservatism
in the United States, we're talking about

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.960
we're not talking about what things were like
pre French Revolution.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.380
What were my understanding is that conservatism
is basically talking about sort of like the

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:58.180
1950s where you still had there was less awareness
of and opposition to not only sometimes systemic,

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:05.899
but actually codified racism out and out sort
of glorification of patriarchy, even crony

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:10.299
capitalism in some ways, where it was more
open, where maybe now crony capitalism is

00:01:10.299 --> 00:01:15.439
in some sense more more hidden, that that's
what I understand as classical conservatism

00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:16.729
being in the United States.

00:01:16.729 --> 00:01:22.009
And sometimes it's presented as we want so-called
family values based on the Bible, as well

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:27.609
as a low, very low regulation environment
which goes towards that crony capitalism thing

00:01:27.609 --> 00:01:30.030
that I said.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:35.969
The basic reality is that as it is, the idea
of conservatism is a form of sleight of hand

00:01:35.969 --> 00:01:42.030
where what they're really talking about is
classical conservatism in many cases is limiting

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:50.639
the structures of power to privileged groups,
whether it's men over women, whether it's

00:01:50.639 --> 00:01:55.759
business elites over workers, whether it's
so so it's not just a class analysis.

00:01:55.759 --> 00:01:57.369
It's not just a race analysis.

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:01.099
That's fundamentally, though, what we mean
by classical conservatism.

00:02:01.099 --> 00:02:08.520
It exists today in some vestiges among certain
Republicans.

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:14.409
It's funny because the people that Trump calls
rhinos, Republicans in name only saying they're

00:02:14.409 --> 00:02:17.709
not really Republicans anymore.

00:02:17.709 --> 00:02:20.709
That's kind of where more classical conservatism
still exists.

00:02:20.709 --> 00:02:23.620
It's people sort of like Mitt Romney.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.560
It's people I mean, I hate to say it.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:33.530
Democratic Senator Joe Manchin is kind of
like a 1950s conservative in some ways.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:37.800
They are ostracized by the Republican Party
now because the Republican Party is no longer

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.909
about the types of issues people like Mitt
Romney care about.

00:02:41.909 --> 00:02:47.390
Even John McCain was sort of beyond what classical
1950s type conservatism was.

00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:54.280
The Republican Party has become about owning
the libs, about rhetoric over substance, about

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:57.780
culture type issues divorced from actual policy.

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:02.469
Even when you look at the more social issues,
even calling them cultural issues instead

00:03:02.469 --> 00:03:07.049
of social as social issues, as interesting
before when it was, you know, gay marriage,

00:03:07.049 --> 00:03:12.269
abortion, et cetera, it linked to policy and
more of a direct way than a lot of these culture

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:13.269
issues.

00:03:13.269 --> 00:03:15.940
When you talk to a lot of these Republicans
who want to fight against cancer culture and

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.420
you say, great, what's the solution?

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.989
There's often really no policy attached to
it because they will quickly realize the policy

00:03:22.989 --> 00:03:28.519
that would come to mind would be outrageously
authoritarian and supposedly against many

00:03:28.519 --> 00:03:29.870
of their stated values.

00:03:29.870 --> 00:03:33.800
So does classical conservatism still exist
in the United States?

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:40.420
To some degree, it's increasingly on the outs
within the Republican Party.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:47.750
It does exist, but it is not predominantly
what the Republican Party today has decided

00:03:47.750 --> 00:03:50.139
to be in favor of.

